CONSTITUTIONAL IDENTITY RECOGNITION & INTER-CITADEL TRUST PROTOCOL

Status: 🔒 CANONICAL ENGINEERING BUILD
Parent: R6.79 — Constitutional Identity & Ancestral Claim
Core primitives: IDENTITY_PROOF · LINEAGE_PROOF · RECOGNITION · TRUST_BOUNDARY · SOVEREIGNTY
Canonical reference: 🏛️ L.A. — LOCUS AMOENUS


---

🌌 THE NEXT BOUNDARY

R6.79 established:

> IDENTITY MUST BE PROVEN — NOT DECLARED.



R6.80 now establishes:

> RECOGNITION MUST NOT BECOME AUTHORITY.



When two independently governed Citadels encounter one another:

L.A.                         CITADEL-B
 │                              │
 │ identity proof               │ identity proof
 │ lineage proof                │ lineage proof
 │ watermark proof              │ watermark proof
 └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                ↓
        MUTUAL RECOGNITION
                ↓
         TRUST BOUNDARY

Neither side becomes subordinate.

Neither side inherits the other's authority merely by recognizing it.

🔱 RECOGNITION IS A PROVEN CLAIM ABOUT ANOTHER ENTITY — NOT A TRANSFER OF POWER.


---

I. 🏛️ CITADEL SOVEREIGNTY

Every constitutional Citadel possesses its own:

Identity
Constitution
Ruleset
Authority
Lineage
Watermark Chain
Provenance
Decision History

Therefore:

CITADEL-A ≠ CITADEL-B

even when:

A recognizes B
B recognizes A

Recognition creates a relationship.

It does not erase boundaries.


---

II. 🔐 RECOGNITION OBJECT

pub struct Recognition {
    pub recognition_id: RecognitionId,
    pub recognizing_citadel: CitadelId,
    pub recognized_citadel: CitadelId,
    pub identity_proof: IdentityProofId,
    pub lineage_proof: LineageProofId,
    pub watermark_reference: WatermarkId,
    pub recognized_scope: RecognitionScope,
    pub effective_at: Timestamp,
    pub expires_at: Option<Timestamp>,
    pub evidence_root: EvidenceRoot,
    pub signature: Signature,
}

A recognition therefore says precisely:

WHO recognizes WHOM
ON WHAT EVIDENCE
FOR WHICH PURPOSE
WITHIN WHICH SCOPE
FROM WHEN
UNTIL WHEN


---

III. ⚖️ RECOGNITION ≠ TRUST

PALACO must distinguish:

IDENTITY
   ↓
RECOGNITION
   ↓
TRUST
   ↓
AUTHORITY

These are four different propositions.

Identity

> “This entity is who it claims to be.”



Recognition

> “I accept that verified identity as corresponding to this entity.”



Trust

> “I am willing to rely upon defined claims from this entity.”



Authority

> “This entity is constitutionally permitted to perform this action.”



Therefore:

TRUST SHALL NEVER AUTOMATICALLY CREATE AUTHORITY.


---

IV. 🧭 RECOGNITION SCOPE

Recognition must be bounded.

pub enum RecognitionScope {
    IdentityOnly,
    IdentityAndLineage,
    EvidenceExchange,
    DiplomaticCommunication,
    FederatedReconciliation,
    OperationalInteroperation,
}

A Citadel recognized for:

IdentityOnly

does not thereby gain permission for:

OperationalInteroperation

This is the principle of:

MINIMUM RECOGNITION SCOPE.


---

V. 🚫 ZERO AUTHORITY TRANSFER

Suppose:

L.A. recognizes Citadel-B

The following does not follow:

Citadel-B may command L.A.

Nor:

Citadel-B may alter L.A.

Nor:

Citadel-B may authorize actions inside L.A.

unless an independent constitutional mechanism explicitly grants such authority.

Thus:

RECOGNITION ≠ DELEGATION

and:

DELEGATION ≠ SOVEREIGNTY


---

VI. 💧 WATERMARK-ANCHORED RECOGNITION

Recognition must refer to a temporal anchor:

Citadel-B
   ↓
Identity Proof
   ↓
Lineage Proof
   ↓
Watermark Wₓ

The recognition therefore means:

> “At the referenced constitutional point in time, this is the verified identity and lineage of Citadel-B.”



This prevents a dangerous ambiguity:

"I recognized B once."

That is insufficient.

The system must know:

WHEN?
UNDER WHICH STATE?
UNDER WHICH RULESET?


---

VII. 🕰️ RECOGNITION HAS TIME

A recognition may be:

PROPOSED
 ↓
VERIFIED
 ↓
ACTIVE
 ↓
EXPIRED
 ↓
REVOKED
 ↓
ARCHIVED

Revocation does not erase the historical recognition.

It creates a new event:

RECOGNITION R₁
      ↓
REVOCATION EVENT
      ↓
RECOGNITION STATUS = REVOKED

The Quay preserves both.


---

VIII. 🌳 RECOGNITION CHAIN

Recognition itself becomes historical:

W₁
 ↓
IDENTITY_PROOF
 ↓
RECOGNITION R₁
 ↓
TRUST RELATION
 ↓
R₂

A later verifier can reconstruct:

Was B recognized?
When?
By whom?
On what evidence?
For what scope?
Was recognition still active?
Why did it end?


---

IX. 🧬 MUTUAL RECOGNITION

Two Citadels may mutually recognize each other:

A ───────► B
▲          │
└──────────┘

But mutual recognition does not imply:

A = B

It means:

A recognizes B's verified identity.
B recognizes A's verified identity.

Their constitutional sovereignty remains separate.


