XIII. 🔒 R6.92 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.92-I01
Material constitutional review SHALL be functionally independent from the original determination where practicable.

R6.92-I02
No actor SHALL automatically validate its own material constitutional decision.

R6.92-I03
Decision, authorization, and verification SHALL remain distinguishable functions.

R6.92-I04
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT convert semantic interpretation into authority.

R6.92-I05
Ambassadors SHALL NOT constitute their own final constitutional verification.

R6.92-I06
TRIAS SHALL verify reviewer authority.

R6.92-I07
TRIAS SHALL evaluate material review independence.

R6.92-I08
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge insufficient review independence.

R6.92-I09
Quay SHALL preserve reviewer provenance.

R6.92-I10
Watermarks SHALL preserve material review transitions.

R6.92-I11
Potential conflicts of interest SHALL be explicitly representable.

R6.92-I12
A conflict indicator SHALL NOT automatically establish substantive falsity.

R6.92-I13
A review whose independence is unproven SHALL NOT silently become final.

R6.92-I14
Reviewer authority SHALL NOT exceed the scope of the review mandate.

R6.92-I15
No reviewer SHALL create constitutional authority merely by reviewing.

R6.92-I16
Review decisions SHALL remain replayable.

R6.92-I17
Review provenance SHALL remain historically preserved.

R6.92-I18
No review mechanism SHALL erase the decision under review.


