XIV. 🔒 R6.93 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.93-I01
Every material evidence object SHALL possess an identifiable provenance root.

R6.93-I02
Evidence identity SHALL remain distinguishable from evidence interpretation.

R6.93-I03
Evidence integrity SHALL be independently verifiable where material.

R6.93-I04
Material custody transitions SHALL be attributable.

R6.93-I05
Material custody transitions SHALL be temporally traceable.

R6.93-I06
Missing custody SHALL remain explicitly represented.

R6.93-I07
Missing custody SHALL NOT be silently inferred.

R6.93-I08
Custody SHALL NOT itself confer constitutional authority.

R6.93-I09
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT manufacture missing evidence provenance.

R6.93-I10
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge evidence custody.

R6.93-I11
TRIAS SHALL verify material custody claims.

R6.93-I12
Quay SHALL preserve evidence custody history.

R6.93-I13
Watermarks SHALL preserve material custody transitions.

R6.93-I14
Evidence with incomplete provenance SHALL remain distinguishable from disproven evidence.

R6.93-I15
A custody defect SHALL NOT silently invalidate unrelated evidence.

R6.93-I16
Evidence provenance SHALL be replayable where required.

R6.93-I17
No custodian SHALL silently modify material evidence.

R6.93-I18
Any authorized material transformation SHALL create an attributable successor record.

R6.93-I19
Evidence handling SHALL NOT rewrite originating historical events.

R6.93-I20
Constitutional conclusions SHALL NOT exceed the integrity and provenance established for their evidence basis.


