XIX. 🔒 R6.100 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.100-I01
Every material constitutional classification SHALL possess explicit criteria.

R6.100-I02
Material classification SHALL be evidence-linked.

R6.100-I03
Determination SHALL be distinguishable from opinion.

R6.100-I04
Classification SHALL NOT be treated as reality itself.

R6.100-I05
BRAINPOWER MAY propose classifications but SHALL NOT independently authorize constitutional effect.

R6.100-I06
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge classification criteria.

R6.100-I07
TRIAS SHALL verify material determination authority.

R6.100-I08
Quay SHALL preserve historical classifications.

R6.100-I09
Reclassification SHALL NOT erase prior classification history.

R6.100-I10
Every material classification SHALL be watermarkable.

R6.100-I11
Every material state SHALL possess traceable lineage where required.

R6.100-I12
Every material determination SHALL identify its evidence basis.

R6.100-I13
Classification SHALL remain temporally contextual.

R6.100-I14
Similarity SHALL NOT automatically establish constitutional equivalence.

R6.100-I15
Categorical membership SHALL NOT automatically confer authority.

R6.100-I16
Absence of sufficient classification evidence SHALL produce an explicit uncertain or unproven state.

R6.100-I17
Alternative classifications SHALL remain discoverable where materially relevant.

R6.100-I18
A determination SHALL expose the distinguishing conditions relevant to its classification.

R6.100-I19
A later determination MAY alter current classification without rewriting historical classification.

R6.100-I20
The constitutional index SHALL remain compatible with continuous evolution.

R6.100-I21
Lineage SHALL answer origin independently from classification.

R6.100-I22
Watermark SHALL provide state identity independently from semantic category.

R6.100-I23
Classification SHALL NOT silently create authority.

R6.100-I24
Classification SHALL NOT silently remove authority.

R6.100-I25
The complete determination path SHALL remain replayable where material.


