XVII. 🔒 R6.101 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.101-I01
Every material constitutional specimen SHALL possess a unique identity.

R6.101-I02
Every material specimen SHALL be associated with a state.

R6.101-I03
Every material specimen SHALL preserve provenance.

R6.101-I04
Every material specimen SHALL preserve its watermark.

R6.101-I05
A preserved specimen SHALL NOT silently mutate when the live state changes.

R6.101-I06
Reclassification SHALL preserve prior classification history.

R6.101-I07
A specimen SHALL NOT imply authority merely through classification.

R6.101-I08
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT autonomously authorize specimen classification with constitutional effect.

R6.101-I09
FOOL SHALL be able to inspect and challenge specimen determination.

R6.101-I10
TRIAS SHALL verify material specimen integrity.

R6.101-I11
Quay SHALL preserve material specimen provenance.

R6.101-I12
A specimen without sufficient evidence SHALL remain explicitly unproven or incomplete.

R6.101-I13
An orphan specimen SHALL NOT be silently accepted as constitutionally legitimate.

R6.101-I14
A historical specimen SHALL remain distinguishable from a current live state.

R6.101-I15
Current state evolution SHALL NOT rewrite preserved historical specimens.

R6.101-I16
Similarity SHALL NOT establish identity.

R6.101-I17
Classification SHALL remain distinguishable from authority.

R6.101-I18
Provenance SHALL remain independently verifiable where required.

R6.101-I19
Material determination SHALL remain replayable.

R6.101-I20
The constitutional herbarium SHALL support continuous evolution without historical erasure.


