XVII. 🔒 R6.103 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.103-I01
Every material constitutional object SHALL possess an explicit type or taxonomic classification.

R6.103-I02
Classification SHALL remain distinguishable from truth.

R6.103-I03
Classification SHALL remain distinguishable from authority.

R6.103-I04
Classification SHALL remain distinguishable from permission.

R6.103-I05
Linguistic synonymy SHALL NOT automatically create constitutional equivalence.

R6.103-I06
Ambiguous terminology SHALL remain explicitly ambiguous until determined.

R6.103-I07
BRAINPOWER MAY propose classifications but SHALL NOT autonomously create constitutional effect.

R6.103-I08
Material classification SHALL be evidence-linked where required.

R6.103-I09
Material classification SHALL be attributable.

R6.103-I10
Material classification SHALL be temporally contextualized where applicable.

R6.103-I11
Taxonomy versions SHALL remain identifiable.

R6.103-I12
Reclassification SHALL preserve historical classification records.

R6.103-I13
Deprecated classifications SHALL NOT be silently erased.

R6.103-I14
Taxonomic evolution SHALL NOT rewrite historical provenance.

R6.103-I15
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge material classifications.

R6.103-I16
TRIAS SHALL verify material classification integrity.

R6.103-I17
Quay SHALL preserve material classification history.

R6.103-I18
Watermarks SHALL distinguish successive classification states where required.

R6.103-I19
A taxonomy SHALL NOT imply authority merely by defining a category.

R6.103-I20
No classification SHALL exceed its evidence, authority, scope, or temporal validity.


