XX. 🔱 R6.105 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.105-I01
A specimen SHALL remain distinguishable from its classification.

R6.105-I02
A specimen SHALL remain distinguishable from its interpretation.

R6.105-I03
Observation SHALL remain distinguishable from inference.

R6.105-I04
Inference SHALL remain distinguishable from determination.

R6.105-I05
A finalized observation SHALL NOT be silently mutated.

R6.105-I06
Reclassification SHALL preserve the originating specimen.

R6.105-I07
Historical classifications SHALL remain discoverable where material.

R6.105-I08
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT convert inference directly into constitutional effect.

R6.105-I09
FOOL SHALL be able to request the underlying specimen.

R6.105-I10
TRIAS SHALL verify material specimen provenance.

R6.105-I11
Quay SHALL preserve material specimen lineage.

R6.105-I12
Watermarks SHALL anchor specimen provenance where required.

R6.105-I13
Taxonomy evolution SHALL NOT corrupt historical observations.

R6.105-I14
Current classifications SHALL NOT silently overwrite historical classifications.

R6.105-I15
Missing specimen provenance SHALL remain explicitly detectable.

R6.105-I16
Missing observation SHALL not be silently reconstructed as fact.

R6.105-I17
Derived specimens SHALL identify their source where causally relevant.

R6.105-I18
Specimen identity SHALL remain stable across ordinary reinterpretation.

R6.105-I19
Constitutional state transitions SHALL remain distinguishable from knowledge updates.

R6.105-I20
No mechanism SHALL destroy a material historical specimen merely because its interpretation changed.


