XVIII. 🔱 R6.106 CANONICAL INVARIANTS

R6.106-I01
Material observations SHALL be contextually bounded.

R6.106-I02
Context SHALL remain distinguishable from assumption.

R6.106-I03
Observed context SHALL remain distinguishable from inferred context.

R6.106-I04
Historical context SHALL remain preserved where material.

R6.106-I05
Current context SHALL NOT silently overwrite historical context.

R6.106-I06
Material contextual claims SHALL possess evidence where required.

R6.106-I07
Temporal validity SHALL be explicit where relevant.

R6.106-I08
BRAINPOWER SHALL NOT silently fill missing context.

R6.106-I09
FOOL SHALL be able to challenge contextual assumptions.

R6.106-I10
TRIAS SHALL verify material contextual integrity.

R6.106-I11
Quay SHALL preserve material contextual history.

R6.106-I12
Watermarks SHALL anchor material contextual transitions where required.

R6.106-I13
Context changes SHALL remain distinguishable from object identity changes.

R6.106-I14
Context changes SHALL remain distinguishable from constitutional state changes.

R6.106-I15
Unknown context SHALL remain explicitly unknown.

R6.106-I16
Incomplete context SHALL NOT silently become complete context.

R6.106-I17
Historical determinations SHALL use historically valid contextual evidence.

R6.106-I18
Later knowledge SHALL NOT silently contaminate earlier determinations.

R6.106-I19
Context lineage SHALL remain replayable where constitutionally material.

R6.106-I20
No mechanism SHALL fabricate historical context.


