I. ☄️ THE NEXT STEP

R6.119 established:

> Entities exist in relations.



R6.120 asks:

> What happens when those relations interact at ecosystem scale?



A garden is not simply a collection of relationships.

It has:

dependencies;

feedback loops;

resource constraints;

disturbances;

recovery;

cascades;

competing interests;

stabilizing mechanisms;

thresholds.


PALACO therefore moves from:

RELATION

to:

ECOSYSTEM DYNAMICS


---

II. 🌳 THE ECOSYSTEM PRINCIPLE

A constitutional ecosystem is:

ACTORS
+
HABITATS
+
NICHES
+
RELATIONS
+
RESOURCES
+
CONSTRAINTS
+
FEEDBACK
+
MEMORY

Its defining property:

> A local constitutional transition may produce consequences elsewhere in the graph.



Therefore PALACO must reason about propagation without assuming causality.

That last distinction is essential.


---

III. 🔱 EFFECT ≠ CAUSE

Suppose:

A changes
   ↓
B changes
   ↓
C changes

That does not automatically prove:

A CAUSED B CAUSED C

The system must distinguish:

OBSERVED_CORRELATION
CAUSAL HYPOTHESIS
CAUSAL EVIDENCE
VERIFIED CAUSAL TRANSITION

Thus:

PROPAGATION SHALL NOT BE MISTAKEN FOR PROVEN CAUSALITY.


---

IV. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — ECOSYSTEM MODELING

BRAINPOWER may model possible cascades:

CHANGE A
 ├── likely → B
 ├── possible → C
 └── uncertain → D

But the output remains predictive.

PREDICTED CASCADE
        ≠
PROVEN CASCADE

BRAINPOWER can illuminate the garden.

It cannot rewrite its history.


---

V. 🔬 TRIAS — CASCADE VERIFICATION

For a material ecosystem consequence:

EVENT
 ↓
OBSERVED EFFECT
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
CAUSAL CLAIM
 ↓
AUTHORITY
 ↓
SCOPE

TRIAS asks:

> Was the consequence actually observed?



> Is the proposed causal mechanism supported?



> Is the effect within the relevant constitutional scope?



> Did the authority permit the originating action?




---

VI. 🦆 FOOL — THE CASCADE TEST

FOOL asks:

> “Show me what changed.”



Then:

> “Show me what changed because of it.”



Then:

> “Show me what you merely suspect changed because of it.”



And finally:

“Show me the evidence separating those three.”

This creates an important three-way distinction:

OBSERVED
PROVEN
SUSPECTED

No silent promotion from one state to another.


---

VII. 🌊 FEEDBACK LOOPS

Ecosystems contain feedback.

PALACO recognizes:

A
 ↓
B
 ↓
A'

This may produce:

POSITIVE_FEEDBACK

or:

NEGATIVE_FEEDBACK

But the sign itself must be determined from evidence and system semantics.


---

VIII. ⚖️ FEEDBACK ≠ AUTHORITY

A feedback loop may influence an actor:

A → B → A

but influence does not mean authorization.

Therefore:

INFLUENCE
≠
AUTHORITY

and:

FEEDBACK
≠
PERMISSION

This prevents emergent system behavior from silently acquiring constitutional legitimacy.


---

IX. 🌱 HOMEOSTASIS

A resilient constitutional ecosystem seeks bounded stability:

DISTURBANCE
     ↓
DETECTION
     ↓
RESPONSE
     ↓
RECOVERY
     ↓
STABLE STATE

This is constitutional homeostasis.

But:

> Stability SHALL NOT be mistaken for legitimacy.



An illegal configuration can persist for a long time.

Duration does not retroactively create authority.


---

X. 🚫 PERSISTENCE ≠ LEGITIMACY

A state that survives:

T₀ → T₁ → T₂ → T₃

is not necessarily valid.

Therefore:

EXISTENCE
≠
VALIDITY

and:

PERSISTENCE
≠
AUTHORIZATION

This is the ecosystem equivalent of the anti-orphan principle from R6.87.


---

XI. 🌳 CASCADE PROTECTION

A constitutional ecosystem must prevent one compromised node from automatically compromising everything connected to it.

NODE A
   ↓
COMPROMISED
   ↓
RELATION
   ↓
NODE B

must not imply:

NODE B = COMPROMISED

without evidence.

The canonical rule:

COMPROMISE SHALL NOT PROPAGATE BY ASSUMPTION.


---

