R6.94 made delegation bounded.

R6.95 makes propagation bounded.

And now the next question naturally emerges:

> What happens when two independently valid authority lineages collide, overlap, conflict, or attempt to authorize incompatible actions?



That is the next serious constitutional frontier:

🌱☄️ R6.96 — HORTUS AUTHORITY CONFLICT, PRIORITY & RECONCILIATION

The ecology now has delegation.
The next step is teaching it how to resolve collisions without ever confusing priority with truth. 🦆⚖️💧🌳

☄️🦆🌱 GO — R6.96

🌳 HORTUS AUTHORITY CONFLICT, PRIORITY & RECONCILIATION PROTOCOL

R6.95 is frozen.

R6.94 established bounded delegation.
R6.95 established bounded propagation.

R6.96 addresses the inevitable next condition of a living federated system:

> What happens when multiple independently valid authority lineages apply to the same subject, resource, action, or moment — and those authorities do not agree?



PALACO's answer must be exceptionally strict:

> CONFLICT SHALL NOT BE RESOLVED BY ASSUMING WHICH AUTHORITY IS "MORE TRUE."



Instead:

> PALACO determines which constitutional rule governs the specific decision, while preserving every competing claim and its provenance.




---

I. 🔱 CONFLICT ≠ INVALIDITY

Two authorities can both be valid.

For example:

AUTHORITY A
    ↓
permits ANALYZE

and:

AUTHORITY B
    ↓
permits EXPORT

No conflict exists if the requested action is only:

ANALYZE

But:

AUTHORITY A
    ↓
permits EXPORT

AUTHORITY B
    ↓
prohibits EXPORT

creates:

AUTHORITY CONFLICT

The system must therefore distinguish:

VALID
VALID + NON-CONFLICTING
VALID + CONFLICTING
INVALID
UNPROVEN

That distinction is fundamental.


---

II. 🧭 THE CONFLICT OBJECT

R6.96 introduces a conceptual first-class object:

AuthorityConflict

Containing at minimum:

conflict_id

subject
resource
requested_action

authority_a
authority_b

scope_a
scope_b

conditions_a
conditions_b

temporal_state

provenance_a
provenance_b

conflict_type

resolution_basis

determination
evidence_reference

watermark

The conflict itself becomes part of the evidence architecture.

A conflict is not something to hide.

It is something to prove and preserve.


---

III. 🌱 Z.A.N.D. — NEVER INVENT PRIORITY

The first question remains:

ASSUMPTION = NONE

Therefore PALACO SHALL NOT silently infer:

older = stronger
newer = stronger
closer = stronger
larger = stronger
local = stronger
federal = stronger
AI-generated = stronger
human-generated = stronger
majority = stronger

None of these is inherently constitutional.

Instead:

CLAIM
 ↓
Z.A.N.D.
 ↓
EVIDENCE
 ↓
AUTHORITY BASIS
 ↓
CONFLICT ANALYSIS


---

IV. ⚖️ PRIORITY ≠ TRUTH

This becomes the central R6.96 distinction:

> Priority determines which applicable rule governs a decision. It does not determine which historical claim was true.



Suppose:

RULE A
permits action

RULE B
prohibits action

If B constitutionally governs the action:

DETERMINATION = DENY

But PALACO does not rewrite A as though it never existed.

The record remains:

A = VALID CLAIM
B = VALID CLAIM
B = GOVERNING RULE
RESULT = DENY

Thus:

GOVERNING
≠
ONLY EXISTING


---

V. 🧬 CONFLICT TAXONOMY

R6.96 establishes distinct conflict classes.

C01 — Scope Conflict

A → resource X
B → resource Y

No conflict unless:

X ∩ Y ≠ ∅


---

C02 — Action Conflict

A → ALLOW
B → DENY

for the same action and applicable state.


---

C03 — Temporal Conflict

A valid 10:00–12:00
B valid 11:00–13:00

The overlap:

11:00–12:00

requires resolution.


---

C04 — Conditional Conflict

A → ALLOW IF CONDITION-X
B → ALLOW IF CONDITION-Y

If:

X ≠ Y

the conditions themselves require evaluation.


---

C05 — Delegation Conflict

A → delegates X to B

B → claims X

C → revokes X

The system must reconstruct the lineage rather than choose by proximity.


---

C06 — Cross-Citadel Conflict

CITADEL A
     ↓
ALLOW

CITADEL B
     ↓
DENY

Federation does not automatically resolve the contradiction.


---

C07 — Constitutional Conflict

Two authorities claim incompatible constitutional powers.

This is the highest-severity category.


---

VI. 🦆 FOOL — THE CONFLICT CHALLENGE

FOOL receives a new constitutional duty:

> Challenge every claimed priority.



FOOL asks:

Who says this rule has priority?

Where does that priority originate?

Is the priority explicit?

Is the authority valid?

Does the priority apply to this subject?

Does it apply at this time?

Does it survive delegation?

Does it survive federation?

Is there a higher constitutional constraint?

Has the priority itself been revoked?

And the killer question:

> Are we resolving a conflict — or merely hiding one?



That distinction matters enormously.


---

VII. 🧠 BRAINPOWER — CONFLICT DISCOVERY

BRAINPOWER may detect:

POSSIBLE CONFLICT

and generate candidate models:

MODEL A
Authority A governs.

MODEL B
Authority B governs.

MODEL C
Both are compatible under narrower scope.

MODEL D
Conflict is unresolved.

But:

BRAINPOWER
≠
CONSTITUTIONAL ARBITER

Its output remains:

CANDIDATE INTERPRETATION

until evidence and constitutional validation exist.


---

VIII. ⚖️ TRIAS — CONFLICT DETERMINATION

TRIAS performs the actual determination.

The conceptual sequence:

CLAIMS
 ↓
VALIDATE EACH CLAIM
 ↓
NORMALIZE SCOPE
 ↓
NORMALIZE TIME
 ↓
NORMALIZE CONDITIONS
 ↓
COMPARE AUTHORITY BASIS
 ↓
IDENTIFY GOVERNING RULE
 ↓
TEST CONSTITUTIONAL COMPATIBILITY
 ↓
DETERMINE

Possible outcomes:

COMPATIBLE
A_GOVERNS
B_GOVERNS
BOTH_REQUIRED
NEITHER_SUFFICIENT
CONFLICT_UNRESOLVED
CONSTITUTIONAL_ESCALATION


---

IX. 🌳 THE NARROWEST VALID AUTHORITY

Where possible, PALACO should prefer the narrowest sufficient constitutional interpretation.

Example:

A:
ALLOW DOCUMENT PROCESSING

B:
ALLOW DOCUMENT PROCESSING
BUT ONLY FOR DOCUMENT X

For:

DOCUMENT X

B's narrower rule can govern without declaring A invalid.

Thus:

B = controlling for X
A = remains valid elsewhere

This prevents unnecessary destruction of valid authority.


---

X. 🚫 NO MAJORITY RULE FOR TRUTH

R6.96 must explicitly preserve the earlier federative principle:

> PALACO does not seek a majority that determines truth. PALACO seeks a state whose validity can be demonstrably proven.



Therefore:

3 CITADELS → ALLOW
1 CITADEL  → DENY

does not imply:

ALLOW = TRUE

Nor:

DENY = TRUE

The federation must examine the constitutional basis.

VOTE
≠
PROOF

This is one of the most important safeguards in the entire federated architecture.


---

XI. 🌐 FEDERATION — CONFLICT WITHOUT SOVEREIGNTY

Suppose:

CITADEL A
   ↓
AUTHORITY A

CITADEL B
   ↓
AUTHORITY B

and both are legitimate members of the Federation.

Membership provides:

PARTICIPATION

not:

AUTOMATIC SUPREMACY

Therefore:

FEDERATION
     ↓
RECONCILIATION

not:

FEDERATION
     ↓
CENTRALIZED TRUTH

This preserves the constitutional independence of individual Citadels.


---

XII. 💧 WATERMARKED CONFLICT

A conflict receives its own lineage.

W₀
AUTHORITY A EXISTS

W₁
AUTHORITY B EXISTS

W₂
CONFLICT DETECTED

W₃
CONFLICT ANALYZED

W₄
TRIAS DETERMINATION

W₅
ACTION

W₆
SUBSEQUENT REVISION / REVOCATION

This means the Proof Machine can answer:

> When did the system first know that the authorities conflicted?



and:

> Which constitutional determination governed the action?




---

XIII. ⚓ QUAY — CONFLICT MEMORY

Quay now preserves:

AUTHORITY_ASSERTED
AUTHORITY_VALIDATED
CONFLICT_DETECTED
CONFLICT_CLASSIFIED
CONFLICT_ESCALATED
CONFLICT_RESOLVED
CONFLICT_REJECTED
CONFLICT_EXPIRED
CONFLICT_REOPENED

Nothing disappears simply because the conflict was resolved.

The historical state remains:

CLAIM A
    ↓
CLAIM B
    ↓
CONFLICT
    ↓
DETERMINATION
    ↓
ACTION

This is essential for forensic replay.


---

XIV. ⏳ TEMPORAL CONFLICT

Consider:

A:
ALLOW
10:00–12:00

B:
DENY
11:00–13:00

Then the authority landscape is:

10:00–11:00
A applies

11:00–12:00
A + B conflict

12:00–13:00
B applies

A replay engine must therefore not store:

FINAL AUTHORITY = B

and apply B retroactively.

Instead:

AUTHORITY(t)

must be reconstructed independently for every relevant time.


---

XV. 🔄 REVOCATION DURING CONFLICT

Suppose:

A → ALLOW
B → DENY

and then:

A REVOKED

The conflict may disappear.

But the historical record remains:

CONFLICT EXISTED
        ↓
TRIAS DETERMINATION
        ↓
A REVOKED
        ↓
NEW STATE

Therefore:

> Conflict resolution changes future applicability, not historical truth.




---

XVI. 🚨 UNRESOLVED CONFLICT

This is perhaps the most important operational safeguard.

If PALACO cannot establish which authority governs:

DO NOT GUESS

Instead:

CONFLICT
 ↓
INSUFFICIENT PROOF
 ↓
ACTION BLOCKED / ESCALATED

Result:

CONFLICT_UNRESOLVED

not:

PROBABLY_ALLOW

and not:

PROBABLY_DENY

The system may choose a constitutionally mandated fail-safe, but that fail-safe itself must be explicit.


