W₅
POST-CUTOVER VERIFICATION

The critical invariant:

> There SHALL be an identifiable constitutional boundary between Cₙ and Cₙ₊₁.



No ambiguous interval.


---

XIII. ⏳ THE CUTOVER MOMENT

Suppose:

Cₙ
valid until:
12:00:00

Cₙ₊₁
valid from:
12:00:00

Then:

ACTION 11:59:59
→ Cₙ

ACTION 12:00:00
→ Cₙ₊₁

The exact temporal semantics must be deterministic.

No:

11:59-ish

No:

CURRENT VERSION

The Proof Machine must know:

CONSTITUTION(t)


---

XIV. 🚦 IN-FLIGHT ACTIONS

This is one of R6.99's hardest cases.

Suppose:

ACTION START
11:59:59

ACTION COMPLETE
12:00:03

What Constitution applies?

PALACO cannot leave this ambiguous.

The constitutional transition protocol must explicitly define the action's governing temporal model.

For example:

EXECUTION_AUTHORIZATION_TIME

may establish the relevant authority state.

Or the Constitution may explicitly define another deterministic rule.

The key invariant is:

> In-flight behavior SHALL NEVER be resolved by implementation convenience.



The governing rule must be explicit and replayable.


---

XV. 🔄 ABORTED MIGRATION

Suppose:

Cₙ
 ↓
MIGRATION
 ↓
POST-CUTOVER CHECK
 ↓
FAIL

The system must distinguish:

MIGRATION_FAILED

from:

HISTORICAL_ERASURE

The event remains.

Cₙ
 ↓
ATTEMPTED Cₙ₊₁
 ↓
FAILURE
 ↓
Cₙ REMAINS GOVERNING

unless the transition had already validly completed.

Thus:

> A failed migration is itself historical state.




---

XVI. 🚨 ROLLBACK ≠ TIME TRAVEL

If Cₙ₊₁ fails operationally and the system returns to Cₙ:

Cₙ
 ↓
Cₙ₊₁
 ↓
ROLLBACK
 ↓
Cₙ

this must not be represented as though Cₙ₊₁ never existed.

Correct:

Cₙ
 ↓
Cₙ₊₁
 ↓
ROLLBACK EVENT
 ↓
Cₙ₊₂

where Cₙ₊₂ may explicitly restore prior governance semantics.

This is crucial.

> Rollback restores a governance configuration; it does not reverse history.




---

XVII. ⚓ QUAY — MIGRATION MEMORY

Quay receives:

MIGRATION_PROPOSED
MIGRATION_ANALYZED
MIGRATION_VALIDATED
MIGRATION_BLOCKED
MIGRATION_STARTED
MIGRATION_CUTOVER
MIGRATION_VERIFIED
MIGRATION_FAILED
